    {
      "id": "0005",
      "front": "What is the method of differences, and when does it apply?",
      "back": "It applies when a general term can be written as $f(r) - f(r + 1)$, so that summing cancels all the interior terms and leaves only $f(1) - f(n + 1)$.",
      "answerType": "SELF_GRADE",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "the term splits into a difference of consecutive values",
          "synonyms": [
            "f(r) - f(r+1)",
            "telescoping"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "interior terms cancel",
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "only the first and last survive",
          "synonyms": [
            "f(1) - f(n+1)"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "acceptedAliases": [
        "telescoping series"
      ],
      "explanation": "It is the standard way to sum $\\frac{1}{r(r+1)}$, which splits as $\\frac{1}{r} - \\frac{1}{r+1}$.",
